# Inspect and finish

Read this complete workflow before the first rendered preview of the final build. It governs self-inspection, independent review, documentation and delivery.

### Inspection budget

Before the first screenshot, plan the viewports and interaction states together, including the motion checks from [animate.md](animate.md) when applicable. Keep the initial evidence in `.phoenix-ui/review/inspect/` and post-fix evidence in `.phoenix-ui/review/confirm/`. The actions already taken determine the stage, even if a command failed or wrote into the wrong directory. Before another product edit or rendered check, read the current batch's evidence (on the web, its capture report) and choose the applicable row below.

| What has already happened | Allowed next work |
|---|---|
| No final-build inspection yet | Capture the planned viewports and states into `inspect/`. |
| Inspection started; product files have not changed since it | Complete and read the planned screenshots, sequence sheets and detection results. Repair failed capture steps without editing product files. Then apply the collected product findings in one batch, without interleaving rendered checks. |
| Product files changed after inspection; no post-fix check yet | Finish that fix batch, then capture once into `confirm/`. An incomplete initial capture does not change this transition. |
| A post-fix rendered check has occurred | Read its evidence and record resolved, remaining or unchecked items. Repair only invalid/missing capture evidence as described below, then hand off to the finish reviewer. No further self-directed product edits. |

Two inspection rounds is the ceiling. Splitting captures by viewport, state, tool or command, or overwriting `inspect/`, cannot turn a post-fix check into another initial inspection. Preserve the first batch. Remaining product findings after confirmation belong in the reviewer handoff.

### Capture the planned states

Inspect the surface's target sizes in one batched screenshot round: desktop and mobile on the web; on a native platform (`ios` / `android` / `adaptive`), the shipped device classes per OS, captured from the simulator or emulator the way the platform reference's Verifying the build section describes.

When the harness reports the user's actual viewport (an in-app browser's size, a named resolution), add that width to the set: the width that breaks is the one the user sees first.

On the web the initial batch is one capture run: `.cursor/skills/phoenix-ui/scripts/phoenix-ui capture <artifact URL or path> --viewport desktop,mobile[,user-<width>=<width>x<height>] --type full --states .phoenix-ui/review/states.json --out .phoenix-ui/review/inspect`. For the confirmation batch, change `--out` to `.phoenix-ui/review/confirm`; keep the first batch as the record of what prompted the fix. Host-browser screenshots and checks belong to the same current batch. Pass the selected batch's actual screenshot and report paths in the handoff below.

`states.json` declares the states the signature interaction and the main flows produce (open and closed, error, success), each reached from a fresh page by its own steps; the signature interaction's state adds `"frames": 8`, and a Persuade surface adds `--mask --thumbs` to the command. A state on an app shell or `100vh` workspace, where a stitched full page distorts, sets `"type": "viewport"`. The default names are the files below: `desktop.png`, `mobile.png`, `user-<width>.png`, and `<viewport>-<state>.png` per state.

On a comp-led build, run `.cursor/skills/phoenix-ui/scripts/phoenix-ui comp-diff --comp <approved comp> --build <current-batch>/desktop.png --spec .phoenix-ui/build/spec.json --out-dir .phoenix-ui/review/diff/final` and read its region rows and paired crops as the critique: the side-by-side is the view the build thread never has on its own, and a region it scores missing or contradicted is a fix whatever the page looks like from memory. Never judge fidelity from one full-page thumbnail; it hides exactly the failures that matter.

On a Persuade surface, verify the mode did its job: a first-time visitor should know what this is, why it matters, and what to do within seconds, in the form's own vocabulary; on the web, judge that from the masked glance thumbnails (`*.mask.thumb.png`), where only layout, imagery, and weight can speak.

A capture is evidence only when it is valid, and you validate before you send. Settle or disable entrance motion first: an element hidden by animation timing reads as a missing element and gets fixed into a regression. Capture full-page shots from the document top. Capture the comp comparison at the comp's own pixel dimensions. Then open every file once and confirm it shows what its name claims: no black or blank regions, no wrong section behind a right filename, no half-loaded state.

On the web, capture runs the mechanical half of this itself: a shot its report marks invalid (written as `<name>.invalid.png`) and a state it lists under `failures` never go to the reviewer. Repair the capture configuration (`--settle-ms` or `--reduced-motion`, the step, the shot type) and recapture only those with `--only`, keeping product files unchanged. For example, a button-name mismatch calls for correcting the capture selector, not editing the button or fixing a CSS finding before the missing states are seen. If product files have already changed after inspection, the next render is confirmation even when some initial states failed. It cannot tell whether a picture is the state its name claims, so the open-every-file check stays yours.

When capture exits 1 (no browser, launcher unavailable), capture the same files with the harness browser under these rules and mark them `host capture` in the packet and the delivery; when neither can render the page, spawn no reviewer and claim neither a review nor a ship: the delivery carries the label `未取证` (`not captured` in an English session) verbatim, followed by the reason. A malformed capture sent onward costs the whole round; the reviewer answers it with `disposition: recapture` and nothing it reviewed binds.

A shot's `findingsNotes` in the capture report are not findings: each names a URL and passage whose font source the page scan could not verify, and the absence of a font finding on that passage is not evidence the font was verified. Read them passage by passage and clear each inside the existing rounds, by recapturing once fonts have settled or by checking that passage's rendered font by hand, and record the basis. The notes and each basis travel to the finish reviewer in the same capture packet; a note still open at the finish is listed in the delivery as unverified, with its URL, passage, and reason. Notes add no disposition word, no blocking gate, and no detector round.

On the web, where this harness runs no design hook, run `.cursor/skills/phoenix-ui/scripts/phoenix-ui detect --json` on the changed source targets once during the initial inspection. Collect its mechanical findings with the screenshots and sequence sheets; read all planned evidence before applying the single fix batch. Findings left after confirmation go to the reviewer. The `findings` capture records per shot are this round's page detection; do not run `detect <url>` on top of them. A native platform skips the detector entirely: it reads HTML and CSS and has no verdict on native code, so the reviewer's floor check is the only slop gate and the input packet says so. Capture the screenshots into the current `inspect/` or `confirm/` batch under `.phoenix-ui/review/`, one file per captured viewport (on the web, `desktop.png` and `mobile.png`, plus `user-<width>.png` whenever the user's viewport joined the inspected set; on native, one per device class, such as `phone.png` and `tablet.png`, suffixed per OS on adaptive), creating that directory when the harness does not; the paths you pass the reviewer are its spec, every viewport you inspected is named required in the packet, and that directory is where it looks when a passed path is missing.

### Hand off the finished inspection

Then spawn the shipped finish reviewer, `phoenix-ui-finish-reviewer` (`phoenix_ui_finish_reviewer` in codex; `/phoenix-ui-finish-reviewer` in Cursor; on GitHub Copilot say "Use the phoenix-ui-finish-reviewer agent"), with the original request, confirmed answers, the artifact path, the screenshot paths (on the web, also the selected batch’s `capture-report.json` and, per file sent, its shot type, plus for each `findingsNotes` entry the basis that cleared it or a line saying it is still open; sequence frames, masks, thumbnails, marks, and sheets are labeled derived evidence that never replaces a state screenshot), the direction contract, existing hook findings, the QUALITY BAR card and approved comp paths (a code-led build has no approved comp; the chosen decision comp rides in that slot as the critique reference, named as such), on a comp-led build the build state (`.phoenix-ui/build/state.json`), the spec, and the diff directories (`.phoenix-ui/review/diff/hero/` and `.phoenix-ui/review/diff/final/`, whose side-by-side, heatmap, region pairs, and `report.json` are the fidelity evidence), and, when the hero step replaced defective comp text, its replacement list, which the delivery also lists entry by entry, the craft-floor reference path, and on a native platform the platform reference path(s), [ios.md](ios.md) / [android.md](android.md), both on adaptive, plus one line saying no detector ran, so the reviewer judges in the platform's conventions rather than the web's.

The reviewer has no browser; screenshots you fail to pass are checks it cannot run. Never read the shipped agents' definition files before spawning; the harness loads them at spawn, and you owe only the input packet.

For state-changing motion, the packet also carries [animate.md](animate.md), the motion timeline, and the implementation paths. Ask the reviewer to read that reference and compare the implemented start, direction, follower offsets and feel parameters with its rules, not only with your direction contract. The sequence is visual evidence; the implementation supplies timing that sparse frames cannot resolve. Keep normal-speed feel explicitly unverified unless the hands-on check occurred.

The review packet is the handoff: keep its product files and evidence unchanged while the reviewer works. Use the wait to draft the delivery from the existing evidence, including the motion report and next steps; do not spend it on new checks or product edits. Wait on any agent with one long timeout rather than a loop of short polls. Resume changes only through the returned disposition below, so the verdict applies to the version actually reviewed.

Verify the return carries the five contract sections (a recapture return carries one, its recapture list); on an empty or thrashed return, respawn once with the same inputs.

This review never runs inside the build thread and never inherits it: spawn the reviewer fresh, with no forked conversation history (`fork_turns: 0` in codex); a reviewer that inherits your transcript inherits your framing, your optimism, and your abstractions, and everything it needs travels in the inputs above.

Only a harness with no subagent capability at all substitutes a fresh in-thread pass after stepping fully out of the build context, run from [degraded/finish-reviewer.md](degraded/finish-reviewer.md), and a substituted or failed-and-replaced review is disclosed in one line at finish, never silently.

When the web surface has a main flow (filling and submitting a form, a multi-step flow, an action that saves data), spawn the shipped UX walker in the same round as the reviewer: `phoenix-ui-ux-walker` (`phoenix_ui_ux_walker` in codex; `/phoenix-ui-ux-walker` in Cursor; on GitHub Copilot say "Use the phoenix-ui-ux-walker agent"), fresh, never forked (`fork_turns: 0` in codex). The handoff carries only the page entry, the main viewport, test credentials as environment variable names or a test file, the safety boundary (test accounts only; no writes to or destruction of real data; no real business accounts in the handoff), and the output directory `.phoenix-ui/review/walk/`. Never give it the original request, user stories, the expected path, source paths, design notes, the direction contract, or earlier findings: it is a first-time visitor, and each of those is a hint it must not have. Pure visual tweaks, direction first viewports, and surfaces with no main flow get no walk, and their delivery carries no walk conclusion. When the harness cannot spawn an isolated subagent, or the walker returns that it could not operate the page, the delivery carries the label `未完成 UX 走查` (`UX walk not completed` in an English session) verbatim, followed by why; walking the path yourself is not an independent walk. Sort each returned issue as a reproducible product problem, a tool or data problem, or an unreproduced guess. A reproducible stuck or wrong turn joins the reviewer's fix batch; after the fix, walk the same path on the real page yourself (rerun capture on the walker's state file in `.phoenix-ui/review/walk/`, or use the harness browser) and record the result, and claim no finish before that. A hesitation is a suggestion. Another walk is not required. When the user chooses to ship with walk issues open, list them.

### Continue from the review

Act on the disposition word; there are exactly four. **recapture**: the evidence failed, not the build. Recapture what the return names under the capture-validity rules, then run a full review over the new evidence. A review conducted on invalid evidence binds nothing, and a verdict pass may never follow it. **rebuild**: fidelity failed wholesale, not in patches. Skip the fix batch and execute the rebuild immediately: re-derive the named regions, produce the named assets, and send the result back for a fresh full review, never a verdict pass; a rebuild replaces regions wholesale, so the whole matrix runs again over the recaptures. Tell the user what is happening rather than asking permission to fix a failure. Consult the user only on a second rebuild directive, both verdicts on the table, or when rebuilding would discard content the user approved. **ship**: nothing is owed; report the verdict at its scope and continue to the documenter. **fix**: apply the material fixes in one batch, rebuild once, and recapture the same viewports over the same files (on the web, `--only` the states the batch touched, and name each file reused unchanged in the verdict packet). A recapture measures positions, loading, and overflow; it cannot measure whether a fix reached the quality the finding named, so send the recaptured screenshots back to the same reviewer for a verdict scoring every material fix resolved, partial, or unresolved (through the harness's agent continuation; without one, run the scoring fresh from [degraded/finish-reviewer.md](degraded/finish-reviewer.md)'s Verdict Pass). Fixes scored partial or unresolved get another batch, recapture, and verdict. Two rounds is the budget an unattended run ends at; an attended session's ceiling belongs to the user, so when the second verdict still lists open items, put the table in front of them and let them choose between shipping as it stands and funding another round. Whoever decides, stop the moment a round resolves nothing, and the reviewer's findings are the only list you work from, never your own re-opened hunt. Do not run a second detector.

A rebuild and a fix round share one asset rule: a raster either round creates or replaces is still asset work under [visualize.md](visualize.md)'s Produce section and keeps its **provenance** like every build raster, and a raster the round abandons is deleted in the same batch. Before either round's result goes back for review or verdict, run `.cursor/skills/phoenix-ui/scripts/phoenix-ui embed-prompt --scan <asset-dir...>` over the directories the artifact's rasters ship from and clear every file it reports by embedding what it is missing: the exact generation prompt for a produced raster, the origin for a sourced, stock, or pre-existing one. The scan only reads; deletion is reserved for rasters the round abandoned, never for a file the scan flagged.

When `.phoenix-ui/media/sources.json` exists, run `.cursor/skills/phoenix-ui/scripts/phoenix-ui media audit <product-paths...>` over the actual product source and shipped asset paths before sending the result for review or reporting it to the user. Clear its findings: replace copied media with imagery permitted in the product, and remove media-area references and attribution from product files. Record the audit result in the delivery; if a `credit` finding is unrelated product copy that you retain, state the basis for that decision.

Report the final verdict under the reviewer's own disposition word and at its actual scope. A verdict pass scores the listed fixes and nothing else: "the reviewer scored all three fixes resolved" is a claim it supports, "no material issues remain" is not. A table with open material findings is never announced as a pass, never softened, and never dressed as whole-surface approval when only a fix list was scored. The UX walk never changes the disposition word. When the user answers a ship with evidence against it, their own screenshot, a named mismatch with the comp, that evidence outranks every capture you made: put their material in the packet and spawn a fresh reviewer for a new full review. Patching inline and self-certifying is how a rejected page ships twice.

After the last correction, spawn the shipped documenter, `phoenix-ui-documenter` (`phoenix_ui_documenter` in codex), with the project root, artifact path, direction contract, PRODUCT.md, [document.md](document.md), and write boundary. Without subagents, load [degraded/documenter.md](degraded/documenter.md) and [document.md](document.md) before writing. Verify the outcome: new worlds and approved system changes require token-bearing DESIGN.md **and** `.phoenix-ui/design.json`, not prose alone. Ordinary extensions compare the finished build against the incumbent system, preserve its files, and report the evidence checked; report pre-existing drift without repairing it unasked. Recheck after later edits. Finish only when review and documentation are complete. In the delivery, the review line opens with the reviewer's last disposition word exactly as it returned it (`ship`, `fix`, `rebuild`, or `recapture`, untranslated in any session language), then its scope; a paraphrase alone loses the word the user acts on. When a walk was dispatched, the UX walk gets its own section: whether it ran, its issues with their sorting and handling, the isolation identifier (subagent ID, session ID, or run directory), and where its evidence is.

On a comp-led build, record the final review disposition with `.cursor/skills/phoenix-ui/scripts/phoenix-ui build-phase finish --disposition <ship|fix|rebuild|recapture>` before the final response. A refused `ship` is an unfinished build; report the outstanding phase with the verdict.

Close the delivery the way [routing.md](routing.md)'s `Next steps after a delivery` describes.
